WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.533 size:95% align:center
PRP is a communication protocol that connects applications through
cryptographic

00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:08.166 size:95% align:center
identities.

00:00:08.446 --> 00:00:12.359 size:95% align:center
They establish relationships and control access permissions.

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:17.801 size:95% align:center
Communication can be direct or pass through aggregators: network
participants that

00:00:17.801 --> 00:00:21.555 size:95% align:center
bring together and forward communications for other participants.

00:00:21.835 --> 00:00:26.804 size:95% align:center
That role does not give them access to content protected between
the endpoints.

00:00:27.084 --> 00:00:32.970 size:95% align:center
How does this architecture protect against impersonation,
monitoring, and attacks?

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:38.360 size:95% align:center
Copying a name or address is not enough to impersonate another
identity.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.822 size:95% align:center
The peer proves control of its key.

00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:44.876 size:95% align:center
A person's or company's identity can be verified separately.

00:00:45.726 --> 00:00:49.731 size:95% align:center
On protected channels, the provider carries encrypted data.

00:00:50.011 --> 00:00:52.681 size:95% align:center
It does not receive the conversation's keys.

00:00:52.961 --> 00:00:58.047 size:95% align:center
Authentication and sequence checks reject tampering and
unauthorized replay.

00:00:58.897 --> 00:01:02.043 size:95% align:center
Different relationships can use different identities.

00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:06.630 size:95% align:center
An aggregator knows its local peers and forwarding context.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:12.214 size:95% align:center
It does not need to know the protected conversation's original
source or final

00:01:12.214 --> 00:01:12.622 size:95% align:center
destination.

00:01:12.902 --> 00:01:18.185 size:95% align:center
A rendezvous is an intermediary where the paths built by the two
endpoints meet.

00:01:18.465 --> 00:01:23.395 size:95% align:center
Each endpoint can know its own path to that meeting point without
receiving the

00:01:23.395 --> 00:01:24.804 size:95% align:center
other side's complete path.

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:29.690 size:95% align:center
Joining the two sides does not require revealing the complete
end-to-end

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.365 size:95% align:center
trajectory to any participant.

00:01:31.645 --> 00:01:37.285 size:95% align:center
With opaque packets of constant size sent at fixed intervals, real
activity no

00:01:37.285 --> 00:01:39.888 size:95% align:center
longer changes the observable traffic pattern.

00:01:40.168 --> 00:01:45.954 size:95% align:center
Cover traffic maintains the same pattern with or without real
data, removing those

00:01:45.954 --> 00:01:48.179 size:95% align:center
variations as clues for correlation.

00:01:49.029 --> 00:01:50.736 size:95% align:center
A path can be blocked.

00:01:51.016 --> 00:01:56.188 size:95% align:center
When reachable alternatives exist, participants can choose other
intermediaries,

00:01:56.188 --> 00:01:59.061 size:95% align:center
including networks in other countries.

00:01:59.341 --> 00:02:04.008 size:95% align:center
Carrying a segment does not require knowing the protected final
identity.

00:02:04.288 --> 00:02:07.272 size:95% align:center
This resistance depends on available paths.

00:02:08.122 --> 00:02:10.943 size:95% align:center
DDoS protection has two fronts.

00:02:11.223 --> 00:02:16.814 size:95% align:center
Admission challenges, proof of work, and state limits constrain
the processing and

00:02:16.814 --> 00:02:18.677 size:95% align:center
memory spent on requests.

00:02:18.957 --> 00:02:24.789 size:95% align:center
To protect subsequent links, each participant enforces forwarding
authorization

00:02:24.789 --> 00:02:26.086 size:95% align:center
before retransmitting.

00:02:26.366 --> 00:02:30.882 size:95% align:center
Rejected traffic need not travel across the network to the victim.

00:02:31.732 --> 00:02:35.888 size:95% align:center
Being recognized does not grant unrestricted access.

00:02:36.168 --> 00:02:39.198 size:95% align:center
Permissions belong to the relationship and service.

00:02:39.478 --> 00:02:45.005 size:95% align:center
Access to one resource does not open the others, and changing
paths does not

00:02:45.005 --> 00:02:46.584 size:95% align:center
silently expand that authorization.

00:02:47.434 --> 00:02:52.903 size:95% align:center
The result is less trust placed in the path, verifiable peer
identity, and

00:02:52.903 --> 00:02:54.585 size:95% align:center
explicit limits on traffic.

00:02:54.865 --> 00:02:55.899 size:95% align:center
PRP.
